# Line endings are a correctness concern here, not a formatting preference.
#
# The repository is authored on Windows and deployed to Linux. Without this file
# Git uses the machine's core.autocrlf, so a shell script or a systemd unit
# checked out on a Windows host and then deployed arrives with CRLF endings. That
# is not cosmetic:
#
#   * `set -Eeuo pipefail\r` makes bash read the variable as `pipefail` plus a
#     stray carriage return, and every later line in deploy.sh dies on
#     `$'\r': command not found`.
#   * `StandardError=append:/var/log/sonvudev-store.error.log\r` gives systemd a
#     log path with a carriage return in it, so `systemctl restart` fails on a
#     file it cannot find.
#
# Neither failure is visible on the machine that wrote the file, which is why
# this has to be enforced by the repository rather than by remembering.

# Anything the shell or an init system executes has to stay LF.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
Caddyfile   text eol=lf

# Source is checked out with native endings. Nothing in the build or the test
# suite depends on either choice: the gates that read source as text already
# split on /\r?\n/ and the indentation-anchored patterns put the \r after the
# value. Declaring it stops the two forms from drifting into the repository.
*.js        text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
*.cjs       text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.css       text eol=lf
*.html      text eol=lf
*.svg       text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf
*.example   text eol=lf
.gitignore  text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# Binaries must never be touched, in either direction.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.ico       binary
*.woff2     binary
